1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,333
Sweetheart, close your eyes.

2
00:00:02,513 --> 00:00:05,309
It's the Saturday after Thanksgiving,

3
00:00:05,309 --> 00:00:06,820
nineteen fifty-five.

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,661
I'm eleven years old.

5
00:00:08,841 --> 00:00:10,724
It's snowing just a little,

6
00:00:10,724 --> 00:00:13,166
and I'm holding my mother's hand on

7
00:00:13,166 --> 00:00:15,887
the corner of Main Street in Mansfield,

8
00:00:15,887 --> 00:00:16,236
Ohio.

9
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:18,495
And we're about to walk into a

10
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:20,782
place that doesn't exist anymore.

11
00:00:20,962 --> 00:00:23,574
Downtown, at Christmas.

12
00:00:23,754 --> 00:00:24,892
Come with me.

13
00:00:25,241 --> 00:00:27,530
You've only ever known the mall,

14
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:29,389
or a box that comes to the

15
00:00:29,389 --> 00:00:29,746
door.

16
00:00:29,926 --> 00:00:32,092
So you can't know what it was

17
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:34,481
like when the whole town came to

18
00:00:34,481 --> 00:00:35,975
the same few blocks,

19
00:00:35,975 --> 00:00:36,871
all at once,

20
00:00:36,871 --> 00:00:38,737
dressed up in their best,

21
00:00:38,737 --> 00:00:40,305
for one month a year.

22
00:00:40,485 --> 00:00:42,610
I'll take you store by store.

23
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:45,751
And I'll tell you what everything cost,

24
00:00:45,751 --> 00:00:47,724
and what Santa said to me.

25
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:50,726
And why my brother Danny got lost.

26
00:00:51,076 --> 00:00:54,079
First, you didn't just go downtown,

27
00:00:54,079 --> 00:00:55,023
sweetheart.

28
00:00:55,203 --> 00:00:56,584
You got ready.

29
00:00:56,764 --> 00:00:58,884
My mother put on her good wool

30
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:01,498
coat, her hat with the little feather

31
00:01:01,498 --> 00:01:02,558
and her gloves.

32
00:01:02,738 --> 00:01:04,641
White gloves in winter,

33
00:01:04,641 --> 00:01:05,965
can you imagine.

34
00:01:06,145 --> 00:01:08,159
Danny had to wear a tie,

35
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:11,347
and he complained the entire bus ride.

36
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:14,230
I wore my patent leather Mary Janes

37
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:15,543
with white socks,

38
00:01:15,543 --> 00:01:16,856
even in the snow.

39
00:01:17,036 --> 00:01:19,555
My toes were frozen by noon.

40
00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:21,035
I didn't care.

41
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:23,107
We took the city bus,

42
00:01:23,107 --> 00:01:24,665
fifteen cents each,

43
00:01:24,665 --> 00:01:27,288
because my father had the car at

44
00:01:27,288 --> 00:01:28,108
the plant.

45
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:30,334
The bus was so full of mothers

46
00:01:30,334 --> 00:01:32,789
with shopping bags that Danny had to

47
00:01:32,789 --> 00:01:33,744
sit on my lap.

48
00:01:33,924 --> 00:01:36,191
And when we got off downtown,

49
00:01:36,191 --> 00:01:38,770
the first thing you heard was the

50
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:40,333
Salvation Army bell.

51
00:01:40,513 --> 00:01:42,092
A man in a uniform,

52
00:01:42,092 --> 00:01:44,751
ringing, next to his red kettle.

53
00:01:44,931 --> 00:01:47,304
My mother always gave me a dime

54
00:01:47,304 --> 00:01:48,147
to drop in.

55
00:01:48,497 --> 00:01:50,618
Before we went in anywhere,

56
00:01:50,618 --> 00:01:52,583
we looked at the windows.

57
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:55,963
Every big department store decorated its front

58
00:01:55,963 --> 00:01:57,494
windows for Christmas,

59
00:01:57,494 --> 00:01:59,650
and they kept it a secret until

60
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:01,529
the day after Thanksgiving.

61
00:02:01,709 --> 00:02:03,717
Then they pulled the curtains.

62
00:02:03,897 --> 00:02:05,942
There was a crowd three deep on

63
00:02:05,942 --> 00:02:06,800
the sidewalk.

64
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:09,453
Children were lifted up on their fathers'

65
00:02:09,453 --> 00:02:10,056
shoulders.

66
00:02:10,407 --> 00:02:12,567
And inside those windows,

67
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:15,074
sweetheart, everything moved.

68
00:02:15,254 --> 00:02:17,576
Little elves hammering toys.

69
00:02:17,756 --> 00:02:20,561
A train going around and around through

70
00:02:20,561 --> 00:02:21,425
cotton snow.

71
00:02:21,605 --> 00:02:24,681
Reindeer whose heads went up and down.

72
00:02:24,861 --> 00:02:26,788
A whole Santa's workshop,

73
00:02:26,788 --> 00:02:28,716
with a clock on the wall.

74
00:02:28,896 --> 00:02:30,906
I would press my nose on the

75
00:02:30,906 --> 00:02:33,563
glass until my mother pulled me away.

76
00:02:33,743 --> 00:02:36,341
Grown men stood there just as long

77
00:02:36,341 --> 00:02:37,029
as I did.

78
00:02:37,379 --> 00:02:40,499
The day after Thanksgiving there was always

79
00:02:40,499 --> 00:02:42,821
a parade, and that was how Santa

80
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:43,764
came to town.

81
00:02:43,944 --> 00:02:45,430
The high school band,

82
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:48,119
marching in the cold with their cheeks

83
00:02:48,119 --> 00:02:48,402
red.

84
00:02:48,582 --> 00:02:51,417
Floats made of chicken wire and paper

85
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:52,030
napkins.

86
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:54,625
The Shriners in their little cars.

87
00:02:54,805 --> 00:02:56,735
And at the very end,

88
00:02:56,735 --> 00:02:58,278
on a fire truck,

89
00:02:58,278 --> 00:03:00,304
with its siren going,

90
00:03:00,304 --> 00:03:02,234
Santa Claus himself,

91
00:03:02,234 --> 00:03:02,909
waving.

92
00:03:03,089 --> 00:03:06,108
And every child on Main Street screamed.

93
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:08,378
Then, when it got dark,

94
00:03:08,378 --> 00:03:10,465
the mayor threw a switch,

95
00:03:10,465 --> 00:03:13,388
and every light on Main Street came

96
00:03:13,388 --> 00:03:14,306
on at once.

97
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:17,648
Big colored bulbs strung across the street

98
00:03:17,648 --> 00:03:19,681
from one side to the other,

99
00:03:19,681 --> 00:03:21,940
and a tall tree in the square.

100
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:24,114
The whole crowd said ooh,

101
00:03:24,114 --> 00:03:26,508
all together, like one person.

102
00:03:26,688 --> 00:03:30,077
Sweetheart, I've seen fireworks since then,

103
00:03:30,077 --> 00:03:32,757
and nothing ever looked as good as

104
00:03:32,757 --> 00:03:33,782
those lights.

105
00:03:34,132 --> 00:03:35,979
Then the revolving door,

106
00:03:35,979 --> 00:03:37,981
and that warm air hit you,

107
00:03:37,981 --> 00:03:39,136
and that smell.

108
00:03:39,316 --> 00:03:42,098
Perfume from the cosmetics counter,

109
00:03:42,098 --> 00:03:44,563
roasted nuts and the floor wax.

110
00:03:44,743 --> 00:03:47,026
The ceiling was two stories high,

111
00:03:47,026 --> 00:03:49,724
with garlands hanging all the way down.

112
00:03:49,904 --> 00:03:51,349
And there was music,

113
00:03:51,349 --> 00:03:54,023
a lady playing Christmas carols on an

114
00:03:54,023 --> 00:03:55,686
organ on the mezzanine.

115
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:56,957
Every year.

116
00:03:57,137 --> 00:03:59,238
I thought she lived there.

117
00:03:59,589 --> 00:04:01,117
And to go upstairs,

118
00:04:01,117 --> 00:04:02,886
you took the elevator.

119
00:04:03,066 --> 00:04:04,250
Not a button.

120
00:04:04,430 --> 00:04:06,604
There was a lady in a uniform

121
00:04:06,604 --> 00:04:07,953
with white gloves,

122
00:04:07,953 --> 00:04:10,427
sitting on a little stool inside,

123
00:04:10,427 --> 00:04:12,450
and she ran it with a brass

124
00:04:12,450 --> 00:04:12,975
handle.

125
00:04:13,155 --> 00:04:15,338
She'd call out every floor.

126
00:04:15,518 --> 00:04:18,710
Second floor, ladies' coats and hats.

127
00:04:18,890 --> 00:04:21,979
Third floor, men's furnishings.

128
00:04:22,159 --> 00:04:25,212
Fourth floor, toys and Santa Claus.

129
00:04:25,392 --> 00:04:28,009
Every child in town knew the fourth

130
00:04:28,009 --> 00:04:28,457
floor.

131
00:04:28,807 --> 00:04:31,163
Here's something you'll find funny.

132
00:04:31,343 --> 00:04:32,426
When you paid,

133
00:04:32,426 --> 00:04:35,674
the salesgirl didn't open a cash register.

134
00:04:35,854 --> 00:04:37,934
She put your money and the sales

135
00:04:37,934 --> 00:04:39,950
slip in a little metal tube and

136
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:41,705
stuck it into a pipe in the

137
00:04:41,705 --> 00:04:42,030
wall.

138
00:04:42,210 --> 00:04:42,872
Whoosh.

139
00:04:43,052 --> 00:04:45,172
It shot off across the store,

140
00:04:45,172 --> 00:04:46,634
through the ceiling,

141
00:04:46,634 --> 00:04:48,242
to an office upstairs.

142
00:04:48,422 --> 00:04:50,049
And a minute later,

143
00:04:50,049 --> 00:04:53,217
thunk, it came back with your change.

144
00:04:53,397 --> 00:04:55,115
Pneumatic tubes.

145
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:57,153
I thought it was magic.

146
00:04:57,503 --> 00:04:59,558
Oh, the toy floor.

147
00:04:59,738 --> 00:05:01,874
Sweetheart, you can't imagine.

148
00:05:02,054 --> 00:05:04,560
There was a whole Lionel train layout

149
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:06,254
on a table as big as this

150
00:05:06,254 --> 00:05:08,828
room, with mountains and tunnels and a

151
00:05:08,828 --> 00:05:11,470
little town with lights in the windows.

152
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:13,965
The boys stood around it like it

153
00:05:13,965 --> 00:05:14,761
was church.

154
00:05:14,941 --> 00:05:18,273
A Lionel set cost about fifty dollars.

155
00:05:18,453 --> 00:05:20,980
That was two weeks of my father's

156
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:21,286
pay.

157
00:05:21,466 --> 00:05:23,939
Most boys just looked.

158
00:05:24,289 --> 00:05:27,671
That year, every boy in America wanted

159
00:05:27,671 --> 00:05:28,561
one thing.

160
00:05:28,741 --> 00:05:30,308
A coonskin cap.

161
00:05:30,488 --> 00:05:33,980
Davy Crockett was on Disneyland on television,

162
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,410
and the song was on every radio.

163
00:05:36,590 --> 00:05:38,649
Davy, Davy Crockett,

164
00:05:38,649 --> 00:05:41,326
king of the wild frontier.

165
00:05:41,506 --> 00:05:44,102
Stores sold millions of those caps,

166
00:05:44,102 --> 00:05:46,550
and the price of raccoon fur went

167
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:47,811
through the roof.

168
00:05:47,991 --> 00:05:50,051
Danny wore his to church until the

169
00:05:50,051 --> 00:05:52,112
minister asked him to take it off.

170
00:05:52,462 --> 00:05:54,644
For girls, it was dolls.

171
00:05:54,824 --> 00:05:57,489
Baby dolls that drank and wet,

172
00:05:57,489 --> 00:06:00,153
a bride doll in a white dress.

173
00:06:00,333 --> 00:06:01,773
And there was Mr.

174
00:06:01,953 --> 00:06:04,966
Potato Head, which back then came with

175
00:06:04,966 --> 00:06:06,156
only the parts.

176
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:08,186
You had to use a real potato

177
00:06:08,186 --> 00:06:09,970
from your mother's kitchen.

178
00:06:10,150 --> 00:06:12,515
Silly Putty in a plastic egg,

179
00:06:12,515 --> 00:06:13,575
for a dollar.

180
00:06:13,755 --> 00:06:14,832
And a Slinky,

181
00:06:14,832 --> 00:06:17,320
walking down the store stairs,

182
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:18,480
over and over,

183
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:21,382
while a salesman made it look easy.

184
00:06:21,733 --> 00:06:23,672
And then the line for Santa.

185
00:06:23,852 --> 00:06:25,846
It went all the way around the

186
00:06:25,846 --> 00:06:26,510
toy floor.

187
00:06:26,690 --> 00:06:28,931
An hour, sometimes more.

188
00:06:29,111 --> 00:06:31,113
Santa sat on a big red throne

189
00:06:31,113 --> 00:06:33,598
in a little cottage made of plywood,

190
00:06:33,598 --> 00:06:35,462
with fake snow on the roof.

191
00:06:35,642 --> 00:06:37,906
A helper in an elf costume took

192
00:06:37,906 --> 00:06:40,098
your picture with a flashbulb,

193
00:06:40,098 --> 00:06:42,290
pop, and you saw spots for ten

194
00:06:42,290 --> 00:06:42,875
minutes.

195
00:06:43,225 --> 00:06:44,634
When it was my turn,

196
00:06:44,634 --> 00:06:46,748
I climbed up and I asked Santa

197
00:06:46,748 --> 00:06:47,876
for a Toni doll,

198
00:06:47,876 --> 00:06:50,061
the one with the hair you could

199
00:06:50,061 --> 00:06:51,259
give a permanent.

200
00:06:51,439 --> 00:06:53,996
And for my father to stop working

201
00:06:53,996 --> 00:06:55,003
on Saturdays.

202
00:06:55,183 --> 00:06:56,843
Santa got quiet.

203
00:06:57,023 --> 00:06:57,986
Then he said,

204
00:06:57,986 --> 00:07:00,060
I'll see what I can do about

205
00:07:00,060 --> 00:07:01,096
the first one,

206
00:07:01,096 --> 00:07:01,911
sweetheart.

207
00:07:02,091 --> 00:07:04,169
The second one's harder.

208
00:07:04,349 --> 00:07:07,194
He smelled like peppermint and cigarettes.

209
00:07:07,374 --> 00:07:10,002
Years later I found out he was

210
00:07:10,002 --> 00:07:10,265
Mr.

211
00:07:10,445 --> 00:07:12,534
Reese from the hardware store.

212
00:07:12,885 --> 00:07:14,580
And right after Santa,

213
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:17,355
they gave every child a little gift.

214
00:07:17,535 --> 00:07:18,754
A candy cane,

215
00:07:18,754 --> 00:07:20,536
or a coloring book,

216
00:07:20,536 --> 00:07:22,787
or a tiny box with a toy

217
00:07:22,787 --> 00:07:23,444
inside.

218
00:07:23,624 --> 00:07:24,820
Can you believe it?

219
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,133
The store gave you a present just

220
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:27,844
for coming.

221
00:07:28,024 --> 00:07:29,580
Danny got a whistle.

222
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:32,331
My mother regretted that whistle for the

223
00:07:32,331 --> 00:07:33,359
rest of the day.

224
00:07:33,709 --> 00:07:35,334
Now, Danny.

225
00:07:35,514 --> 00:07:37,058
He was eight that year.

226
00:07:37,238 --> 00:07:39,456
While my mother was looking at a

227
00:07:39,456 --> 00:07:41,744
pair of gloves for Grandma Pearl,

228
00:07:41,744 --> 00:07:43,061
he was right there,

229
00:07:43,061 --> 00:07:44,378
and then he wasn't.

230
00:07:44,558 --> 00:07:45,998
Just gone.

231
00:07:46,178 --> 00:07:47,885
My mother turned white.

232
00:07:48,065 --> 00:07:49,288
In those days,

233
00:07:49,288 --> 00:07:51,561
there were no cell phones,

234
00:07:51,561 --> 00:07:52,523
no cameras.

235
00:07:52,703 --> 00:07:55,564
Just a huge store with five floors,

236
00:07:55,564 --> 00:07:57,199
two thousand people,

237
00:07:57,199 --> 00:08:00,387
and an eight-year-old boy in a coonskin

238
00:08:00,387 --> 00:08:00,714
cap.

239
00:08:01,064 --> 00:08:02,671
A floorwalker came over,

240
00:08:02,671 --> 00:08:04,948
that's what they called the man in

241
00:08:04,948 --> 00:08:07,157
the suit who walked the floor and

242
00:08:07,157 --> 00:08:08,564
helped the customers.

243
00:08:08,744 --> 00:08:11,062
He went to a telephone on the

244
00:08:11,062 --> 00:08:13,061
wall, and a minute later,

245
00:08:13,061 --> 00:08:14,740
over the whole store,

246
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:15,780
a voice said,

247
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:16,499
will Mrs.

248
00:08:16,679 --> 00:08:19,229
Walter Miller please come to the toy

249
00:08:19,229 --> 00:08:21,992
department, your son is waiting for you

250
00:08:21,992 --> 00:08:22,983
at the trains.

251
00:08:23,163 --> 00:08:24,824
Of course he was.

252
00:08:25,004 --> 00:08:26,745
Where else would he be.

253
00:08:27,095 --> 00:08:29,155
My mother didn't spank him,

254
00:08:29,155 --> 00:08:32,359
which in nineteen fifty-five tells you how

255
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:33,503
scared she was.

256
00:08:33,683 --> 00:08:36,146
She just held him so tight his

257
00:08:36,146 --> 00:08:37,213
cap fell off.

258
00:08:37,393 --> 00:08:38,894
And on the bus home,

259
00:08:38,894 --> 00:08:41,297
she held his hand the whole way,

260
00:08:41,297 --> 00:08:42,648
and she held mine,

261
00:08:42,648 --> 00:08:44,975
and she didn't let go of either

262
00:08:44,975 --> 00:08:46,852
of us until we got to Elm

263
00:08:46,852 --> 00:08:47,377
Street.

264
00:08:47,728 --> 00:08:49,701
But first, lunch.

265
00:08:49,881 --> 00:08:51,221
On the top floor,

266
00:08:51,221 --> 00:08:52,877
there was a tea room.

267
00:08:53,057 --> 00:08:55,541
White tablecloths, real silver,

268
00:08:55,541 --> 00:08:59,148
waitresses in black dresses with little white

269
00:08:59,148 --> 00:08:59,709
aprons.

270
00:08:59,889 --> 00:09:02,989
Ladies came downtown just to eat there.

271
00:09:03,169 --> 00:09:06,081
My mother ordered the chicken salad on

272
00:09:06,081 --> 00:09:07,230
a lettuce leaf,

273
00:09:07,230 --> 00:09:08,533
sixty-five cents.

274
00:09:08,713 --> 00:09:11,406
Danny and I had grilled cheese and

275
00:09:11,406 --> 00:09:12,753
a chocolate soda.

276
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:15,382
It was the fanciest place I had

277
00:09:15,382 --> 00:09:16,172
ever been.

278
00:09:16,523 --> 00:09:18,875
And a lady came around to the

279
00:09:18,875 --> 00:09:20,823
tables modeling dresses,

280
00:09:20,823 --> 00:09:22,039
from the store,

281
00:09:22,039 --> 00:09:23,175
while you ate.

282
00:09:23,355 --> 00:09:25,738
She'd stop at your table and turn

283
00:09:25,738 --> 00:09:28,481
around, slowly, and tell you the price

284
00:09:28,481 --> 00:09:29,636
and which floor.

285
00:09:29,816 --> 00:09:31,767
My mother said that was how they

286
00:09:31,767 --> 00:09:32,254
got you.

287
00:09:32,434 --> 00:09:34,868
You'd finish your chicken salad and go

288
00:09:34,868 --> 00:09:37,301
straight down to the second floor with

289
00:09:37,301 --> 00:09:38,646
your pocketbook open.

290
00:09:38,996 --> 00:09:41,730
After lunch, my mother gave me one

291
00:09:41,730 --> 00:09:43,097
dollar of my own,

292
00:09:43,097 --> 00:09:44,384
to buy presents,

293
00:09:44,384 --> 00:09:47,118
and let me go into the Woolworth's

294
00:09:47,118 --> 00:09:49,932
by myself while she waited outside.

295
00:09:50,112 --> 00:09:51,297
A whole dollar.

296
00:09:51,477 --> 00:09:53,785
I walked up and down those wooden

297
00:09:53,785 --> 00:09:55,464
floors for half an hour,

298
00:09:55,464 --> 00:09:57,142
adding it up in my head.

299
00:09:57,322 --> 00:09:59,955
A handkerchief for my father,

300
00:09:59,955 --> 00:10:00,863
ten cents.

301
00:10:01,043 --> 00:10:02,721
A comb for Danny,

302
00:10:02,721 --> 00:10:03,806
five cents.

303
00:10:03,986 --> 00:10:06,866
That left eighty-five cents for my mother.

304
00:10:07,216 --> 00:10:09,092
And for my mother I bought a

305
00:10:09,092 --> 00:10:11,637
little blue bottle of Evening in Paris

306
00:10:11,637 --> 00:10:12,173
perfume.

307
00:10:12,353 --> 00:10:15,225
Every girl bought her mother Evening in

308
00:10:15,225 --> 00:10:18,170
Paris, it was the five-and-dime perfume.

309
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:20,544
The lady at the counter wrapped it

310
00:10:20,544 --> 00:10:22,674
in tissue paper for free and tied

311
00:10:22,674 --> 00:10:23,772
it with a ribbon.

312
00:10:23,952 --> 00:10:26,337
My mother wore it to church every

313
00:10:26,337 --> 00:10:27,638
Sunday for a year,

314
00:10:27,638 --> 00:10:29,590
until the bottle was empty,

315
00:10:29,590 --> 00:10:31,903
and she kept the empty bottle on

316
00:10:31,903 --> 00:10:33,855
her dresser until she died.

317
00:10:34,205 --> 00:10:37,188
Now, how did we pay for Christmas?

318
00:10:37,368 --> 00:10:39,183
Not with a credit card,

319
00:10:39,183 --> 00:10:40,050
sweetheart.

320
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:42,529
Most people didn't have one.

321
00:10:42,709 --> 00:10:45,121
My mother had been putting money in

322
00:10:45,121 --> 00:10:47,464
a Christmas Club at the bank since

323
00:10:47,464 --> 00:10:48,015
January.

324
00:10:48,195 --> 00:10:49,388
A dollar a week,

325
00:10:49,388 --> 00:10:51,699
and in November they gave you a

326
00:10:51,699 --> 00:10:53,489
check for fifty dollars.

327
00:10:53,669 --> 00:10:55,933
That was our whole Christmas.

328
00:10:56,113 --> 00:10:57,239
For four people,

329
00:10:57,239 --> 00:10:59,492
and something for Grandma Pearl.

330
00:10:59,842 --> 00:11:01,344
For the big things,

331
00:11:01,344 --> 00:11:02,767
there was layaway.

332
00:11:02,947 --> 00:11:05,310
You picked out the toy in October,

333
00:11:05,310 --> 00:11:07,046
paid a little every week,

334
00:11:07,046 --> 00:11:08,992
and the store kept it in the

335
00:11:08,992 --> 00:11:09,895
back for you,

336
00:11:09,895 --> 00:11:11,562
with your name on a tag.

337
00:11:11,742 --> 00:11:14,237
And then my mother had her books

338
00:11:14,237 --> 00:11:16,107
of S and H Green Stamps.

339
00:11:16,287 --> 00:11:18,940
You got stamps at the grocery store,

340
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:21,150
licked them into little books,

341
00:11:21,150 --> 00:11:23,729
and traded the books for a toaster.

342
00:11:23,909 --> 00:11:27,034
Our toaster cost my mother eight books

343
00:11:27,034 --> 00:11:27,857
of stamps.

344
00:11:28,207 --> 00:11:31,492
We went downtown every Christmas for years.

345
00:11:31,672 --> 00:11:33,738
Then, little by little,

346
00:11:33,738 --> 00:11:34,726
it changed.

347
00:11:34,906 --> 00:11:38,064
Malls started opening out by the highways,

348
00:11:38,064 --> 00:11:40,545
with free parking and a roof over

349
00:11:40,545 --> 00:11:41,373
everything.

350
00:11:41,553 --> 00:11:44,221
The first big indoor mall opened in

351
00:11:44,221 --> 00:11:46,661
Minnesota in nineteen fifty-six.

352
00:11:46,841 --> 00:11:49,522
People liked not having to fight the

353
00:11:49,522 --> 00:11:49,894
snow.

354
00:11:50,074 --> 00:11:51,437
And one by one,

355
00:11:51,437 --> 00:11:54,254
the big stores downtown closed.

356
00:11:54,604 --> 00:11:57,292
Now, when I drive down Main Street,

357
00:11:57,292 --> 00:12:00,749
sweetheart, some of those buildings are still

358
00:12:00,749 --> 00:12:01,210
there.

359
00:12:01,390 --> 00:12:03,004
Some are offices.

360
00:12:03,184 --> 00:12:04,289
Some are empty,

361
00:12:04,289 --> 00:12:06,353
with paper over the windows.

362
00:12:06,533 --> 00:12:08,847
And I stand on the corner where

363
00:12:08,847 --> 00:12:10,639
I held my mother's hand,

364
00:12:10,639 --> 00:12:12,953
and I can still hear that bell.

365
00:12:13,133 --> 00:12:15,721
Whoosh, thunk, fourth floor,

366
00:12:15,721 --> 00:12:17,661
toys and Santa Claus.

367
00:12:18,012 --> 00:12:19,811
Tell me in the comments.

368
00:12:19,991 --> 00:12:22,516
What was the department store in your

369
00:12:22,516 --> 00:12:23,335
town called?

370
00:12:23,515 --> 00:12:25,637
Did you ride the elevator with the

371
00:12:25,637 --> 00:12:27,323
lady with the white gloves?

372
00:12:27,503 --> 00:12:28,989
I'd love to know.

373
00:12:29,169 --> 00:12:31,027
Goodnight, sweetheart.

374
00:12:31,207 --> 00:12:33,799
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

375
00:12:33,799 --> 00:12:35,456
another story tomorrow.
